  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Drier today with decreasing clouds and warmer \ntemperatures.\n\nEarly morning water vapor imagery shows continued southwesterly flow \nacross the Carolinas. A deepening upper low over Quebec is drawing \ndrier air into the western Appalachians with broad northwesterly \nflow extending back all the way to the northern Plains. At the \nsurface, a cold front is making its way through the Ohio Valley with \nsurface high pressure building southward from Alberta CA. Meanwhile \nacross NC, the extensive cloud cover that has been locked in place \nsince Saturday is in the process of breaking up and eroding. While \nthere is no shortage of high cloud cover within the broad \nsouthwesterly flow aloft, the thicker/lower/more stratiform ceilings \nare quickly eroding across the western Piedmont this morning. \n\nFor the remainder of the early morning hours, a weak wave moving \nthrough upstate SC could bring a stray sprinkle or shower to the \nSandhills/southern Coastal Plain, although most ob sites are only \nshowing trace amounts (if that). Given relatively drier air to the \nnorth, most of this precip should remain on the southern fringes of \nour CWA before moving offshore after daybreak. There could be some \npatchy fog north of this area of precip although given extensive \ncloud cover, widespread dense fog isn't expected. \n\nBy late morning/early afternoon, we should see a marked reduction in \ncloud cover as the Ohio Valley cold front approaches the area and \nbroad northwesterly/downslope flow takes hold across NC. This will \nalso allow temps to warm well into the mid/upper 70s this afternoon \nbefore falling into the upper 40s to mid 50s tonight. A reinforcing \nsurge of drier air will arrive late tonight, with dewpoints falling \ninto the mid 40s by daybreak Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A dry week is expected. Temperatures will average \nclose to normal for early October through mid-week, then trend above \nnormal late in the week. The next chance of rain possibly Saturday \nand Sunday.\n\nMuch drier air associated with surface high pressure building from \nthe Great Lakes and Mid-Atlantic states will bring mainly \nclear/sunny conditions Tuesday into Wednesday night. Lows will dip \ninto the 40s Tuesday night and around 50 Wednesday night. Highs will \nbe rather cool in the upper 60s to mid 70s Tuesday and Wednesday. \n\nReinforcing dry air associated with another high pressure will \nspread into the region late Thursday and Friday. The temperatures \nwill most likely trend into the upper 70s to lower 80s late week, \nwith lows in the 50s. \n\nThe next chance of rain appears to be possibly over the weekend. \nThere continues to be quite a bit of model spread with the potential \nGulf to SE United States system, with the EC ensemble currently \npreferred, which may obviously change with time. As of right now, \nthe EC depicts higher rain chances Saturday afternoon into Sunday, \nwith the higher POP in the south and east as is the low pressure \ntrack.",
